  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cam Schlittler just rewrote what winning looks like for this team.",
    "what_this_means": "A 1\u20130 shutout is not the kind of game the Yankees usually win. Twelve strikeouts through 7.1 innings is the kind of pitching that gives a team permission to play small. And they did. Goldschmidt hit one to make it stick. The rest of the lineup went 2 for 13, left four runners on base, and never found the moment. But that's the point: Schlittler's dominance was so complete that the Yankees didn't need an offense. They just needed one run and discipline enough to protect it. Bednar shut the door in the ninth. A 2.07 ERA starter throwing a shutout with a save-ready closer backing him in a one-run game. That's not a formula breaking. That's a formula working at its cleanest. The streak is now two games, and they're two back in a division that doesn't give away room. One run was enough because it had to be.",
    "what_to_watch": "PHI brings TBD to the mound tonight in a game the Yankees are 2.0 back on a two-game streak that matters more with every win in the division folding closer. Ryan Weathers will need to match what Schlittler just proved. That pitching can carry a thin margin.",
